const title = "Ice Maker Not Making Ice? Causes & Fixes — Same Day";
const description = "Ice maker not making ice or not working? Most often it's the water supply, a clogged filter, a raised shut-off arm, or a frozen fill tube. What to check yourself and when it's a tech job.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my ice maker not making ice?",
    a: "Nine times out of ten it comes down to water not reaching the ice maker or the unit not being cold enough to freeze. Start with the basics: the ice maker's shut-off arm or switch may be in the OFF position, the water supply valve may be closed or the line kinked, the water filter may be overdue and clogged, or the fill tube may have frozen solid. On a fridge ice maker, the freezer also has to be cold enough — roughly 0°F to 5°F — to trigger a harvest. When those are all ruled out, the water inlet valve or the ice maker's control module is usually the failed part."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I reset my ice maker?",
    a: "Most ice makers have a test or reset button on the unit itself — a small button on the underside or front of the ice maker head. Pressing it forces a harvest cycle, which is the fastest way to see whether the mechanism runs at all. If your model doesn't have a visible button, cycling the shut-off arm up and down or turning the ice maker off and on in the settings does the same thing on many units. If a forced cycle runs but no water arrives, the problem is upstream — supply, valve, or filter. If nothing moves at all, it's the motor or control module."
  },
  {
    q: "Why did my ice maker stop working after I changed nothing?",
    a: "Two quiet culprits cause this in Southern California. The first is the water filter finally clogging — filters have a life of about six months, and once flow drops below what the ice maker needs, production tapers off and then stops. The second is hard-water scale. LA and the surrounding valleys have hard water, and mineral buildup slowly narrows the fill tube and gums up the valve until it can't deliver a full fill. Both are common, both look like a sudden failure, and both are fixable without replacing the ice maker."
  },
  {
    q: "My undercounter ice maker stopped producing — what's different about it?",
    a: "Standalone and undercounter ice makers (the nugget and gourmet-cube units from brands like Scotsman, U-Line, and Marvel) have parts a fridge ice maker doesn't: their own condenser, a water pump, a reservoir, and often a required periodic cleaning cycle. When one stops, the usual causes are a dirty condenser coil choking the cooling, a clogged water line or pump, or a unit overdue for its cleaning cycle that's now scaled up. Many of these units display a service or cleaning light. They're worth repairing, but the diagnosis is different from a fridge ice maker, and the sealed side is a tech job."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a broken ice maker worth fixing?",
    a: "For an integrated fridge ice maker, almost always — a water valve, a fill-tube thaw, a filter, or a control module is a modest repair against a fridge you're keeping. For a dedicated undercounter or nugget machine, it depends on the fault: a pump, valve, or cleaning-related issue is a clear repair, while a sealed-system problem on an older unit is worth weighing against replacement. Our techs tell you which situation you're in before any work. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

