/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">It's usually water not arriving — or the unit switched off.</h2>

    <p><strong>When an ice maker stops making ice, the cause is almost always upstream of the ice maker itself: the shut-off arm or switch is in the OFF position, the water supply valve is closed or the line is kinked, the water filter is clogged and overdue, or the fill tube has frozen solid. On a fridge ice maker, the freezer also has to sit around 0°F to 5°F to trigger a harvest. Only after those are ruled out is it usually the water inlet valve or the control module. Find the reset/test button and force a cycle to see whether the mechanism runs at all. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>People assume "no ice" means the ice maker died. Our techs find the ice maker is usually fine — it's just not getting water, or it was never turned back on after someone cleaned the freezer. Checking the cheap, upstream things first is exactly how you avoid paying to replace a part that was working the whole time.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WATER-FIRST CAUSES -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Why it happens</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Check the water path, then the machine.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Shut-off arm raised or ice maker switched off</h3>
      <p>Every ice maker has a way to stop itself when the bin is full — a wire bail arm that lifts, or an on/off switch or setting. It's easy to knock the arm up while grabbing ice, or to leave the unit off after cleaning. This is the very first thing to check, and it costs nothing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Closed valve, kinked line, or low pressure</h3>
      <p>The water supply valve behind or under the appliance may be partly closed, and the line can kink when the fridge is pushed back against the wall. Without steady water pressure, the ice maker can't fill. Follow the line from the unit back to the valve and confirm it's fully open and unkinked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Clogged water filter</h3>
      <p>A water filter is good for about six months. Past that it clogs, flow drops, and ice production tapers and then stops — often with no other warning. Replacing an overdue filter is one of the most common fixes for a fridge that "suddenly" stopped making ice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Frozen fill tube</h3>
      <p>The small fill tube that delivers water to the ice mold can freeze into a plug, usually when a slow leak dribbles and refreezes. Thawing it clears the block, but if the tube keeps freezing, the water valve or fridge temperature is the real cause.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Hard-water scale</h3>
      <p>Southern California's hard water leaves mineral scale that slowly narrows the fill tube and coats the valve until it can't deliver a full fill. It builds gradually and then looks like a sudden failure. Descaling and, on undercounter units, running the cleaning cycle addresses it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Water inlet valve or control module</h3>
      <p>When water is confirmed available but still isn't filling the mold, the water inlet valve has usually failed. If a forced cycle won't run at all, the ice maker's motor or control module is the culprit. Both are tech-level replacements — the valve is electrically live and the module drives the whole harvest sequence.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Run through these before calling anyone.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Confirm the ice maker is ON.</strong> Lower the shut-off arm if it's raised, or switch the ice maker on in the controls. Then force a harvest with the test/reset button if your unit has one.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the water supply.</strong> Trace the line to the shut-off valve, open it fully, and straighten any kink. Low or no pressure means no ice.</li>
      <li><strong>Change the water filter.</strong> If it's been more than six months, replace it. This alone restores a lot of ice makers.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify the temperature.</strong> A fridge ice maker needs the freezer around 0°F to 5°F. If the freezer is warm, fix that first — see our freezer guides below.</li>
      <li><strong>Thaw a frozen fill tube.</strong> Gently warm the fill tube (a hair dryer on low or a warm damp cloth) to melt a plug. If it refreezes within days, the valve or temperature needs a tech.</li>
      <li><strong>Undercounter units: run the cleaning cycle.</strong> If your standalone machine has a service/cleaning light, run its cleaning cycle and clear the condenser of dust. A scaled-up unit often revives.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> arm, supply valve, filter, temperature, fill-tube thaw, and cleaning cycles are all safe homeowner work. Do not replace the water inlet valve, open the ice maker's wiring, or touch the sealed refrigeration side of an undercounter unit — those are shock, water, and pressure risks for a tech.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 TECH + INTERNAL LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Water confirmed, still no ice? Now it's diagnostic time.</h2>

    <p>When the arm is down, the supply is open, the filter is fresh, the temperature is right, and there's still no ice, the fault is in the machine: a failed water inlet valve, a dead ice-maker motor or control module, or — on undercounter and nugget units — a water pump, a scaled water line, or a sealed-system problem. Our techs force a harvest, check for water at the valve, and test the module in sequence, so you replace the one part that actually failed rather than the whole assembly.</p>

    <p><strong>Which ice maker do you have?</strong> If yours is built into your refrigerator's freezer, the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> guide covers the fridge-integrated details. This page and the <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/">ice maker repair</a> hub focus on ice makers as their own appliance, including standalone and undercounter units. If you have a Samsung fridge specifically, its ice maker has a documented pattern all its own — see <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/samsung-ice-maker-not-working/">Samsung ice maker not working</a>.</p>

    <p>More help: brand coverage on <a href="/brands/samsung-ice-maker-repair/">Samsung ice maker repair</a> and <a href="/brands/lg-ice-maker-repair/">LG ice maker repair</a>. If ice is building up instead, see <a href="/services/freezer-repair/ice-buildup/">freezer ice buildup</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/ice-maker-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/ice-maker-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/irvine/ice-maker-repair/">Irvine</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Ice maker not making ice: common questions.</h2>

